description: Verify with unit tests, not browser automation; hand UI checks to the user
alwaysApply: true
---

# Verification

Browser automation is slow and expensive, and the user can check the UI in
seconds. Do not use it.

## Never

- The `browser-use` subagent, or `cursor-ide-browser` tools, to verify work.
- Driving the app to confirm a change looks right or a page still loads.

Terminal checks against a running server are fine: `curl` for status codes,
health endpoints, and API payloads. It is the *browser* that is off limits.

## Instead

Push logic out of the UI into something a test can call, then test that.

```ts
// ❌ BAD: only checkable by clicking Download and unpacking the file
async function downloadZip(assets, kind) { /* fetch, zip, click <a> */ }

// ✅ GOOD: layout and bytes are unit-testable; only the <a> click is not
export function planZipEntries(assets, kind): PlannedEntry[]
export function buildArchive(plan, provide): Promise<ArchiveResult>
```

Stub the browser seam (`fetch`, canvas, `URL.createObjectURL`) and assert on
the real output. Prefer `vitest` in this repo.

## Then hand off

When something genuinely needs a human eye, finish the code, run
`npm run typecheck` and `npm test`, and end the turn with a short numbered
checklist of what to click and what to expect. Be specific — name the button,
the panel, and the expected result. Do not ask permission to use the browser.
